The problem is a mismatch between the prefix defined in your Java configuration class and the property names in your application.properties file.
In your OracleDbConfig.java, you are telling Spring to look for properties starting with spring.datasource.oracle:
@ConfigurationProperties(prefix = "spring.datasource.oracle")
public DataSource oracleDataSource() { ... }

However, in your properties file, you are defining them under spring.datasource (without the .oracle part):
spring.datasource.url=${SPRING_DATASOURCE_URL}
spring.datasource.username=${SPRING_DATASOURCE_USERNAME}

Because of this mismatch, Spring Boot creates an empty DataSource without a URL, which causes HikariCP to throw the jdbcUrl is required error.
Since you also have a Hive database configuration (spring.datasource.hive.*), you are using a multi-datasource setup. Therefore, you must namespace your Oracle properties to match the Java code.
The Fix
Update your application.properties (or environment variables mapping) to include .oracle. in the keys. Additionally, HikariCP strongly prefers jdbc-url over url.
Change your properties to this:
# ==========================================
# 1. ORACLE DATABASE CONFIGURATION
# ==========================================
spring.datasource.oracle.jdbc-url=${SPRING_DATASOURCE_URL}
spring.datasource.oracle.username=${SPRING_DATASOURCE_USERNAME}
spring.datasource.oracle.password=${SPRING_DATASOURCE_PASSWORD}
spring.datasource.oracle.driver-class-name=${SPRING_DATASOURCE_DRIVER_CLASS_NAME}

Alternative Fix (If you don't want to change the properties file)
If you want to keep the properties file exactly as it is, you must change the Java code in OracleDbConfig.java to use the base prefix:
    @Primary
    @Bean(name = "oracleDataSource")
    @ConfigurationProperties(prefix = "spring.datasource") // Removed .oracle
    public DataSource oracleDataSource() {
        log.info("ORACLE_DB_INIT | Initializing Primary Oracle DataSource");
        return DataSourceBuilder.create().build();
    }

Recommendation: I highly recommend the first fix (updating the properties file) because it keeps your Oracle configuration clearly separated from your Hive configuration (spring.datasource.hive.*).
